# Relational division
#
# Adapted from the Datalog Educational System (DES) by Fernando Saenz-Perez,
# which offers division as a built-in operator (its manual, section 4.1.14).
#
# "Which employees work on *every* project that smith works on?" That is a
# universally quantified question, and Datalog has no universal quantifier.
# The standard move is to negate twice: instead of "E covers every project",
# ask for "no project that E fails to cover, and none of those exist".
#
# The `missing` rule is where the work happens. It pairs each employee with
# each of smith's projects and keeps the pairs the employee does *not* work
# on, the counterexamples. An employee with no counterexamples divides.
#
# Note that `employee(E)` has to come before `not work(E, P)`: negation
# filters, it never binds, so E and P must already be bound when it runs.

work("smith", "alpha").
work("smith", "beta").
work("jones", "alpha").
work("jones", "beta").
work("jones", "gamma").
work("brown", "alpha").
work("white", "gamma").

employee(E) :- work(E, _).
smith_project(P) :- work("smith", P).

missing(E, P) :- employee(E), smith_project(P), not work(E, P).

covers(E) :- employee(E), not missing(E, _).

?- covers(Employee).

output predicate counterexample(E, P) :- missing(E, P).
